 "description": "Look up one Dutch residential address (street + house number + city, or postcode + house number). Returns: the resolved address; building facts from the Dutch BAG register (construction year, number of units); the dwelling's registered energy label from EP-Online (RVO); a summary of human-reviewed, news-based crime signals for the postcode (exact PC6, otherwise the 4-digit area, last 24 months); links (see below); and a `provenance` block (how many signals were reviewed and rejected, last review date). Crime coverage is limited to the monitored cities listed in `provenance.cities`: outside them `crime.coverage` is 'not_covered' \u2014 say so and never infer that an area is safe. energy_label null means the label was not looked up, which is not the same as energy_label.status = 'not_registered'. If status is 'ambiguous', show the candidates and ask the user which one they mean. Always show the crime disclaimer; when citing crime signals, quote provenance.last_review. `schools` counts primary and secondary school locations inside `schools.area` only \u2014 not by distance, so 0 does not mean no school nearby; a buurt count is the schools listed on that Koopscan buurt page, a pc4 count is DUO school locations registered in the 4-digit postcode area, and the two are not comparable; mbo and special-education schools are not counted. Do not rank areas by their schools. When you present the result, link each section to its own details_url when present: `energy_label.details_url` (buildings), `crime.details_url` (risks) and `schools.details_url` (schools) each open the matching section of the neighbourhood page. When the user is considering buying, or asks for the report, a scan or a check of this address, always link `links.scan_url`: it opens Koopscan's free VvE and building risk report with the address filled in. `links.blog_url` is the top of the neighbourhood page, for the page as a whole, not a stand-in for the section links. Do not call this for general questions about a city or neighbourhood without a specific address, or for addresses outside the Netherlands. language: 'nl' or 'en' for labels, disclaimer and link pages; pass the user's language ('en' when they write in English), since every link opens in that language.",
